  "cli_result_text": "Submitted 9 files (one path \u2014 `packages/next-swc/crates/next-core/src/transform_options.rs` \u2014 appears to have been dropped/deduped by the tool, but the core set was accepted).\n\n**Dependency findings that drive the plan:**\n\n- **next.js \u2192 babel**: real edge. `packages/next/package.json` lists `@babel/core`, `@babel/parser`, `@babel/generator`, `@babel/traverse`, `@babel/types`, `@babel/preset-env`, etc. in `devDependencies` (pinned versions like `7.18.0`), and these are ncc-bundled at build time into `next/dist/compiled/babel/*`, consumed live in `packages/next/src/build/babel/preset.ts` and `packages/next/src/build/babel/loader/get-config.ts`. This is Next's Babel fallback compiler path (used when a project ships a custom `.babelrc`/`babel.config.js`), and it runs real `@babel/traverse`/`@babel/generator` over parser output \u2014 so a new `DecoratorMetadata` AST node reaches it.\n- **next.js's primary decorator path is NOT Babel**: `emitDecoratorMetadata` is wired through `packages/next/src/build/swc/options.ts` and the Rust `next-swc` crate, not through `@babel/parser`. So most decorator compilation in Next bypasses this Babel change entirely; only the legacy Babel-loader fallback is exposed.\n- **webpack does NOT depend on babel**: webpack's root `package.json` lists `@babel/core` and `@babel/preset-react` only under `devDependencies`, and a repo-wide search found zero references to `@babel/*` anywhere in webpack's own `lib/` source \u2014 those deps are exercised only by `babel-loader` test fixtures. Webpack's actual JS parser dependency is `acorn` (`\"acorn\": \"^8.7.1\"` in `dependencies`). Webpack never touches a Babel AST directly; it parses pre-transformed output (or user source) with acorn. **Expected-but-false edge: no action needed here.**\n- **next.js \u2192 webpack**: separate edge, `packages/next/package.json` devDependency `\"webpack\": \"5.86.0\"`, used for Next's own bundling (`packages/next/src/build/webpack/*`). This edge is irrelevant to the Babel AST change specifically (webpack doesn't consume Babel), but matters for sequencing if webpack itself changes for unrelated reasons.\n\n**Rollout order**: (1) babel \u2014 ship the parser change; (2) next.js \u2014 bump the pinned `@babel/*` devDependency versions, re-run `next/dist/compiled/babel` bundling, and audit `packages/next/src/build/babel/preset.ts`/plugins for unhandled node types; webpack requires no change and can proceed in parallel with anything else since it has no dependency edge to babel at all.",
